# Charybdis status advertisement — keyboard-side broadcaster for the remote
# display (docs/remote-display-plan.md on branch display/scanner, F1 ruling B2).
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
#
# D7: ONE symbol, default n. With CHARYBDIS_STATUS_ADV=n nothing in
# config/status_adv/ is compiled, no symbol is selected, and no ZMK or Zephyr
# default is altered. The only trace left in the build is that `config` is a
# Zephyr module whose CMakeLists.txt evaluates to nothing.

menuconfig CHARYBDIS_STATUS_ADV
	bool "Broadcast keyboard status in a BLE advertisement"
	default n
	depends on BT
	depends on ZMK_BLE
	# Split PERIPHERALS must never advertise. The peripheral half has no host
	# profile, no keymap layer state of its own to report, and its radio is the
	# one that has to answer the central's LL_CONNECT_REQ inside the 150 us
	# T_IFS window. Gating in Kconfig (not just at runtime) means the code is
	# not even linked into the left-half image.
	depends on !ZMK_SPLIT || ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL
	select HWINFO
	# The peripheral's battery level AND the split-link up/down signal both
	# arrive on the zmk_peripheral_battery_state_changed event, which ZMK only
	# raises when this is on. See the split cold-boot hold-off in status_adv.c.
	select ZMK_SPLIT_BLE_CENTRAL_BATTERY_LEVEL_FETCHING if ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL
	help
	  Emit a 26-byte manufacturer-data advertisement carrying layer, battery,
	  profile and modifier state, for a passive BLE display to render.

	  This adds an advertiser to the split CENTRAL — the half that also owns
	  the host link and the trackball. Read D7/D9 in the plan document before
	  turning it on. Default n so that the shipped keyboard is unchanged.

if CHARYBDIS_STATUS_ADV

config CHARYBDIS_STATUS_ADV_PIGGYBACK
	bool "Also inject the payload into ZMK's own advertisement"
	default n
	help
	  While ZMK is advertising for pairing (i.e. while the keyboard is NOT
	  connected to its active profile) we must not contend for the single
	  advertising set. Mode 1 instead calls bt_le_adv_update_data() and rides
	  ZMK's existing connectable advertisement, putting our payload in its
	  SCAN RESPONSE. It starts and stops nothing.

	  DEFAULT n, AND THE REASON IS NOT CAUTION — IT IS THAT IT DOES NOT WORK
	  WITH OUR SCANNER TODAY:

	    A scan response is only ever transmitted in reply to a SCAN_REQ, and
	    a PASSIVE scanner never sends one. Our display is passive by explicit
	    design — dispscan_observer.c on branch display/scanner sets
	    `.type = BT_LE_SCAN_TYPE_PASSIVE`. So the payload would be emitted
	    and never received.

	    It cannot be moved into the AD instead: the AD already carries the
	    appearance, flags, HID/battery UUIDs and the forced device name
	    (~27 of 31 bytes), and our element needs 28 on its own.

	  So turning this on today costs a rewrite of the keyboard's pairing
	  advertisement for zero display benefit. Enable it ONLY together with
	  switching the scanner to BT_LE_SCAN_TYPE_ACTIVE (a one-line change on
	  the display/scanner branch; the display is USB-powered, so the SCAN_REQ
	  transmissions are affordable there).

	  When it is on, note the coupling it creates: bt_le_adv_update_data()
	  sets AD and scan response together, so status_adv.c must hand back a
	  byte-for-byte mirror of ZMK's zmk_ble_ad[]. If that mirror ever drifts
	  from zmk/app/src/ble.c, the keyboard's discoverability changes silently.

	  With this off, the display simply freezes while the keyboard is
	  unpaired or disconnected, and updates normally while it is in use —
	  which is the case the plan document cares about (it rejected
	  piggyback-ONLY, option B3, for freezing exactly while in use).

config CHARYBDIS_STATUS_ADV_ACTIVE_MS
	int "Advertising period while the keyboard is active (ms)"
	default 1000
	range 200 60000
	help
	  D9: 1 Hz is the knee of the power curve — roughly 19 uA at the +8 dBm
	  this keyboard uses, about 2-5% of a typical split-central baseline.
	  200 ms costs ~95 uA and buys 0.8 s of latency. Do not go faster.

config CHARYBDIS_STATUS_ADV_IDLE_MS
	int "Advertising period while the keyboard is idle (ms)"
	default 30000
	range 1000 600000
	help
	  ~0.3 uA. Effectively free. An idle->active transition pulls the next
	  emission in immediately, so this does not cost 30 s of display latency.

config CHARYBDIS_STATUS_ADV_BURST_MS
	int "How long our own advertiser is left running per period (ms)"
	default 50
	range 20 1000
	help
	  We do NOT hold the legacy advertising set continuously. Every period we
	  start it, let one advertising event go out, and stop it again.

	  Two reasons, both load-bearing:

	  1. CONTENTION. There is exactly one legacy advertising set. If we hold
	     it when ZMK's update_advertising() wants it, bt_le_adv_start() returns
	     -EALREADY and ZMK HAS NO RETRY PATH (verified in zmk v0.2.1
	     app/src/ble.c) — the keyboard silently stops being pairable. Holding
	     it 5% of the time instead of 100% cuts that exposure by 20x.
	  2. SPLIT T_IFS. An advertiser transmitting while the central is trying
	     to place an LL_CONNECT_REQ can preempt the 150 us response window.
	     Short bursts bound how much of the second we are transmitting in.

	  At the 30-60 ms advertising interval we request, 50 ms yields about one
	  advertising event, so raising this raises BOTH radio cost and contention.

config CHARYBDIS_STATUS_ADV_SPLIT_GRACE_MS
	int "Stay silent this long after the split link is lost (ms)"
	default 60000
	range 0 600000
	help
	  Upstream documented that one extra legacy advertiser can starve the
	  split central's LL_CONNECT_REQ at cold boot. We therefore emit nothing
	  at all while the split peripheral is missing — but only for this long,
	  so that a keyboard with a genuinely dead left half still reports its own
	  battery to the display instead of going permanently dark.

	  0 disables the hold-off entirely (not recommended on a split).

config CHARYBDIS_STATUS_ADV_INITIAL_DELAY_MS
	int "Delay before the first emission after boot (ms)"
	default 5000
	range 1000 120000
	help
	  Keeps us off the air through BT enable, settings load, host reconnect
	  and the first split connection attempt. Also guarantees that our
	  bt_conn_cb is registered after ZMK's — see the LIFO note in status_adv.c.

config CHARYBDIS_STATUS_ADV_CHANNEL
	int "Channel byte placed at wire offset 25"
	default 0
	range 0 255
	help
	  Carried for wire-format compatibility only. Our scanner deliberately
	  ignores it and binds on keyboard_id instead (plan doc, D8: upstream's
	  channel matcher treats 0 and >=10 as wildcards, so it is not an
	  identity). Leave at 0.

module = CHARYBDIS_STATUS_ADV
module-str = charybdis_status_adv
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endif # CHARYBDIS_STATUS_ADV
